    "grade": "7-8",
    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Mr. Cook bought a big box of his favourite Halloween candies to hand out to trick-or-treaters. However, he ate half of all the candies himself before the first child came and took her share. He ate half of what was left before the second child came, and half of what was left again before the third child came and took all of the remaining candies. If it is known that each child received the same number of candies, which of the following statements is **certainly true** about how many candies Mr. Cook originally bought?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "It is a multiple of 3"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "It is a multiple of 4"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "It is a multiple of 6"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "It is a multiple of 7"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "It is a multiple of 11"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Work backwards",
        "body": "Let each child get $c$ candies.\n- The third child took everything: $c$. Before Mr. Cook ate half: $2c$.\n- After the second child there were $2c$, so before her $3c$, and before Mr. Cook ate: $6c$.\n- After the first child $6c$, before her $7c$, and at the start $14c$.\n\nSo he bought $14c$ candies — always a multiple of 7. (With $c = 1$, 14 is not a multiple of 3, 4, 6 or 11.)\n\n**Answer: (D)**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "algebra",
      "number-theory"
    ],
